  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Remaining dry with above normal temperatures, \nthen some rain possible late this week into early next week.\n\nClouds are still expected to trend downward in coverage \nslightly overnight, but there will still be passing clouds here \nand there. Cannot rule out a rogue sprinkle in the northern\nAlleghenies. Overnight lows tonight will be in the 40s.\n\nUpper ridging will build overhead tomorrow as high pressure\nremains in place offshore. Dry conditions and above normal\ntemps are forecast beneath a mixture of clouds and sun. High \ntemps will generally be in the lower to middle 70s, and winds \nwill remain light out of the south. Patchy fog may be possible \ntomorrow night, with lows in the upper 40s to lower 50s.\n\nDry conditions and above normal temps will persist through the \ndaylight hours on Wed. High temps will once again reach well \ninto the 70s, and winds will begin to pick up slightly out of \nthe south. Mostly sunny skies are expected to start the day, but\nhigh clouds will be on the increase during the afternoon and \nevening hours in advance of an upper trough approaching from the\nwest. A sw embedded within the broader trough will lift \nnortheastward through the Great Lakes Sun night. This system has\ntrended further northwest and a bit further away from the area,\nbut a few showers could still be possible by later Wed night.\n\nThe synoptic pattern across the eastern CONUS becomes much more \nactive at the end of this week, through the weekend, and into early \nnext week. A series of large upper troughs over the Midwest are \nforecast to steadily push north and east toward the Mid-Atlantic. \nOne notable change over the last several model cycles is that the \ninitial upper trough does not pass directly through our area, \ninstead slides northeast across the OH Valley into PA. A weaker \nshortwave ahead of the main trough does cross our region Thu, \nand this lkly brings at least scattered showers and a few \ntstorms. However, given that the strongest upper level support \nnow remains north of our area, we probably won't be getting a \nlot of precip like earlier models run depicted. Above normal \ntemps lkly to end the week as highs reach the upper 60s to mid \n70s. More abundant cloud cover on Fri keeps temps a few degrees \nlower than Thu.\n\nThe secondary upper trough and an associated cold front dig into the \nMid-Atlantic Fri afternoon into Fri night. However, it appears \nthis second system will be rather progressive and models \nindicate the surface reflection \"skipping\" the Alleghenies, then\nredeveloping east of I-95. If that occurs we'll see little \nprecip outside of the mtns. Another hindering signal is models \nindicate a potential dry slot ahead of the cold front, which \nalso cuts down on how much shower activity can develop. \n\nMost of the mid-level vorticity associated with this second upper \ntrough cuts off over the central Great Lakes Fri night into \nSat, then pushes well to our north/east Sun. Given the recent \ntrends in the evolution of the synoptic pattern late this week, \nthe NBM PoPs are lkly way too high for Thu and Fri. \n\nLooking ahead at Sunday into early next week, the active pattern \nbrings another strong trough to the region Sun night into \nMon. This system is forecast to be much larger than the \nprevious two, and bring colder air in its wake. Periods of \nshowers are possible at times on Mon, though model timing \nvaries so this could change in the coming days.",
